    <h1>What Your ISP Sees Without a VPN</h1>
    <p class="article-intro">Your internet service provider sees far more than you think. Without a VPN, your traffic is entirely visible to them. Here's what's exposed — and what a VPN actually changes.</p>

    <h2>What Your ISP Sees Without a VPN</h2>
    <p>Your ISP is the mandatory gateway between your device and the internet. Every request passes through their servers. Without encryption, they see:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Every domain you visit</strong> — even on HTTPS sites, the domain name is visible in DNS requests</li>
      <li><strong>Your activity schedule</strong> — when you connect, for how long, how frequently</li>
      <li><strong>Data volume exchanged</strong> — which allows inferring your usage type (streaming, downloading, browsing)</li>
      <li><strong>Your public IP address</strong> — which uniquely identifies your connection</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In France, ISPs are legally required to retain this metadata for one year. It can be requested by authorities.</p>

    <h2>HTTPS Isn't Enough</h2>
    <p>Many people think the HTTPS padlock protects them. It's partially true — the <strong>content</strong> of your exchanges is encrypted. But the <strong>domain name</strong> remains visible, particularly via DNS requests which travel in plain text by default.</p>
    <p>Your ISP can't see what you're reading, but they can see which site you visited, when, and for how long.</p>

    <h2>What a VPN Changes</h2>
    <p>When you use VulcainVPN, all your traffic is encrypted before leaving your device and routes through our France server. Your ISP then sees only one thing: an encrypted connection to our server. No visited domains, no content, no DNS queries.</p>

    <div class="article-highlight">
      <h3>Without VPN vs With VulcainVPN</h3>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Without VPN</strong> — ISP sees: domains visited, schedules, volume, public IP</p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>With VulcainVPN</strong> — ISP sees: encrypted connection to a VPN server. That's it.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What About Speed?</h2>
    <p>That's the real question. Many VPNs slow down your connection considerably — sometimes by 50–80% — because their servers are overloaded by thousands of simultaneous users.</p>
    <p>VulcainVPN works differently: <strong>dedicated server, deliberately limited subscriber count</strong>. If your base internet connection is fast (fibre, 4G/5G), you keep most of that speed through the VPN. Encryption adds a slight unavoidable latency, but no congestion from server overload.</p>
    <p>The simple rule: your VPN speed will be close to your base internet speed — not a tenth of it.</p>

    <h2>DNS Requests: The Often-Forgotten Weak Link</h2>
    <p>Even with HTTPS everywhere, your DNS requests — the "translation" of a domain name to an IP address — often travel in plain text through your ISP's servers. VulcainVPN routes your DNS through the encrypted tunnel too, eliminating this leak.</p>

